diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce an outage at Location C. All four Location C hosts (PC11–PC14) have working local connectivity to their default gateway and working reachability to remote networks.

Evidence:
- From each PC (PC11–PC14), pings to the default gateway 192.168.200.1 succeed with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs (e.g., PC11→192.168.200.1 avg 0.72 ms).
- Location C hosts can reach a remote site host (PC5 at 172.16.10.5) with 0% loss and ~1.6–2.0 ms RTT (e.g., PC11→PC5).
- ARP on PC11 shows a valid neighbor entry for 192.168.200.1 (06:26:54:2e:6c:b5).
- Router4 (the default gateway for 192.168.200.0/24) has eth0 up at 192.168.200.1/24 and is learning/advertising routes via RIP; its routing table includes remote subnets and a default route via 172.25.1.2 (router3 side), indicating upstream reachability is established.

Affected component: none currently; L2 access, gateway interface (router4 eth0), and routing all appear healthy. Mechanism/Localization: Successful ARP, ICMP, and routing entries confirm end-to-end path from Location C LAN (switch4/switch5/switch6) via router4 and upstream. Root cause: No persistent fault found; the reported outage appears to have been transient and is not reproducible. Corrective fix: No action required now. Recommend monitoring router4 and the uplinks (router4–router3 and router4–router5) for flaps; if issue recurs, capture timestamps and interface counters to isolate an intermittent link or process restart. 
=== END ===